# Geodesic thorn testsuite configuration.
#
# Eleven tests.  Nine of them run on a 50^3 KerrSchild grid over
# [-25,25]^3 (dx = 1, Driver::ghost_size = 4, Carpet excision of the
# r < 4 sphere via Geodesic::excised_radius) with dt = 0.1, and the
# Geodesic::step = 5 in eight of the nine.  All eight run 225 Cactus steps
# (particle time 1125 M, grid 22.5 M); circ_orbit_amr_small adds a refined
# box on top of the same base grid.
# circ_orbit alone keeps the original 32 steps of Geodesic::step = 100
# (particle time 3200 M, grid 3.2 M) so that the sparse-dump case stays
# covered.  The
# dump cadence is what the plots turn on: 32 samples over 17.06
# revolutions is 167.8 deg/frame, and joining consecutive samples draws an
# aliased {32/15} star polygon rather than the circle the orbit is, while
# 37.5 samples per revolution is 9.6 deg/frame and draws a circle.
# One test adds a single CarpetRegrid2 box.  The last two run on the CarpetX driver instead of
# Carpet: minkowski_line_carpetx on flat ADMBaseX data, and
# bl_orbit_carpetx on Brill-Lindquist initial data from SpacetimeX.
# (ADMBaseX, the only metric source CarpetX can hand to Geodesic, has no
# Kerr-Schild initial data, and ADMBase cannot be combined with CarpetX at
# all -- see the header of minkowski_line_carpetx.par.)  Both use 64^3
# cells over [-10,10]^3 and dt = 0.1; the flat one runs 32 steps, the
# Brill-Lindquist one 200.  Each test dumps the full particle
# state (t x y z u^t u^x u^y u^z tau cctk_time) once per Cactus step
# (32-33 rows for circ_orbit, 225 for the other Kerr tests, 226 for
# circ_orbit_amr_small, 804 for bl_orbit_carpetx), and the
# driver compares it row by row against the reference in the matching
# directory.
#
# What is covered:
#
#   circ_orbit           Exact = yes: the analytic Kerr-Schild metric and
#                        Christoffel symbols.  One particle on the
#                        prograde equatorial circular orbit r = 11.989
#                        (M = 2, a = 0.5).  Pins the ODE integrator, the
#                        u^mu renormalisation and the dump format.
#
#   circ_orbit_conserved the same run through util/check_conserved, which
#                        prints the Kerr constants of motion (E, L_z) for
#                        every row and compares those two columns; a broken
#                        integrator shows up as a drift of E / L_z.
#
#   circ_orbit_mpi       the same run on 2 MPI ranks.  In Exact mode the
#                        grid safe-region checks are skipped (each rank
#                        owns only part of the grid), so the dump must be
#                        bit-identical to the single-rank reference.  This
#                        is the rank-count independence test.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid      Exact = no: the metric and the Christoffel
#                        symbols come from the ADMBase grid (KerrSchild
#                        initial data in 3+1 form) through the default
#                        local27 sampler -- 5x5x5 node fit plus 6th-order
#                        nodal FD.  Carpet::init_fill_timelevels = yes
#                        makes the stationary-metric detection engage
#                        (d_t g = 0), as for the analytic metric.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_l27  same, metric_interp = "l27": 3x3x3 node fit of
#                        metric and Christoffels, current time level only.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_aei  same, metric_interp = "aei": AEILocalInterp's
#                        pointwise 4th-order Lagrange interpolant.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_carpet
#                        same, metric_interp = "carpet": the same
#                        4th-order interpolant reached through
#                        LocalInterp instead of AEILocalInterp, i.e. the
#                        data-acquisition path rather than the stencil.
#
#   circ_orbit_grid_2p   the circ_orbit_grid run on 2 MPI ranks, with its
#                        own reference: the multi-rank stencil path
#                        (Geodesic's own halo exchange, GEODESIC_HALO_W = 5)
#                        must be reproducible at fixed rank count.
#
#   circ_orbit_amr_small the same orbit on the 50^3 base grid plus one
#                        static CarpetRegrid2 box of half-width 14
#                        (dx = 0.5), on 2 MPI ranks: exercises the
#                        per-level grid acquire and pick_level.
#
#   minkowski_line_carpetx
#                        the CarpetX driver instead of Carpet: four
#                        particles in four octants on a flat ADMBaseX grid,
#                        sampled by CarpetX's own interpolator (the driver
#                        selects it; metric_interp does not apply).  Together
#                        with bl_orbit_carpetx it exercises the CarpetX code
#                        path -- the pointwise ADMBaseX sampler, the two GLOBAL-mode
#                        entry points Geodesics_init_carpetx /
#                        Geodesics_integrate_carpetx, and the multi_cx
#                        replication mode (every rank integrates every
#                        particle, because CarpetX's interpolator is an MPI
#                        collective).  The answer is analytic to roundoff
#                        (straight lines, constant u^mu): the final positions
#                        match x0 + v t to 8e-16.  Unlike the grid-sampler
#                        Kerr tests, this one IS bit-identical at 1, 2 and 4
#                        ranks -- replication carries no chunk boundaries.
#                        Needs CarpetX, ADMBaseX and ODESolvers in the
#                        configuration; the EinsteinToolkit thornlist has
#                        them enabled.
#
#   bl_orbit_carpetx     the CarpetX sampler again, but on a CURVED
#                        stationary metric: SpacetimeX/BrillLindquist
#                        extends ADMBaseX::initial_data with
#                        "Brill-Lindquist" -- gamma_ij = (1+m/2r)^4 delta_ij,
#                        K_ij = 0, alpha = 1/(1+m/2r), beta^i = 0, the
#                        puncture capped at min_radius = 0.25.  It is the
#                        first initial-data thorn that satisfies
#                        CarpetX::SetupGlobals (all REAL grid functions,
#                        only Loop required).  Four particles at r = 6,
#                        tangential coordinate speed 0.28, slightly
#                        super-circular (the circular speed there is
#                        0.237147) so r grows to 6.112 by t = 20; three are
#                        equatorial, one is in the y-z plane.  All four have
#                        the same E and |L|, so r(t) must coincide: it does
#                        to 7.8e-14.  Against a geodesic integrated with the
#                        analytic Christoffel symbols of the same metric the
#                        trajectory agrees to 6.4e-05 in position at
#                        dx = 1/4 and 6.1e-06 at dx = 1/8, and the metric
#                        sampling error at the particle falls 2.2e-7 ->
#                        2.3e-8 -> 9.3e-10 for dx = 1/4, 1/8, 1/16, i.e.
#                        fourth order, matching CarpetX::interpolation_order
#                        = 3.  Bit-identical at 1, 2 and 4 ranks.  Needs
#                        BrillLindquist in the configuration in addition to
#                        CarpetX, ADMBaseX and ODESolvers.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION, and why the grid-sampler references are generated at
# the rank count the test runs at: with Exact = no the trajectory is NOT
# bit-identical across different rank counts.  The grid data itself is
# bit-identical (checked by dumping ADMBase::metric with
# CarpetIOASCII::compact_format = yes, output_ghost_points = no), but the
# chunk boundaries follow the decomposition, so a particle is handed to a
# new owner at a different point along its path and the floating-point
# summation order changes: circ_orbit_grid_2p differs from the
# single-rank circ_orbit_grid by at most 8.4e-3 in x and 8.7e-3 in y
# over the whole 225-row trajectory (8.4e-3 and 8.7e-3 overall, 5.0e-4
# and 8.7e-3 at the final sample, t = 1125; measured 2026-11-09 from the
# reference dumps now in this directory).  Two rank-dependent mechanisms behind the earlier, much
# larger disagreement are now fixed: the halo exchange covers edges and
# corners as well as faces (a node Christoffel whose finite-difference
# window touched an unfilled edge cell used to fall back silently to the
# analytic assembly), and the chunk-boundary constraint is applied even
# to a face the particle sits exactly on (it used to be skipped, letting
# the particle cross the neighbouring subdomain in one step and be
# dropped).  Exact = yes is rank-count independent (circ_orbit_mpi).
# See doc/documentation.tex, "Known limitations".
#
# The heavy runs (100-particle rings, 3-level AMR, the
# TwoPunctures BBH sets, the conv100 sampler comparisons) are in
# test/extra/.  The Cactus testsuite driver globs test/*.par only, so
# they are not run automatically; test/extra/README explains how to run
# them as a local regression set.  They are too big for the testsuite:
# the review criteria are under 10 s per test and under 2 min for the
# whole component, and circ_orbit_amr alone takes ~20 min on one core.

EXTENSIONS txt

TEST circ_orbit
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 1
}

TEST circ_orbit_conserved
{
  POSTPROC check_conserved
  RELTOL 1e-8
  NPROCS 1
}

TEST circ_orbit_mpi
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 1
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_l27
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_aei
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_carpet
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_grid_2p
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST circ_orbit_amr_small
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST minkowski_line_carpetx
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}

TEST bl_orbit_carpetx
{
  RELTOL 1e-12
  NPROCS 2
}
